.P
Table 4-1 shows that SO (0/14) and SI (0/15) are also called LS1 and
LS0, respectively. SO and SI (shift out and shift in) are the
traditional ASCII names or mnemonics. LS1 and LS0 (lock shift G1 and
lock shift G0) are alternate names that are useful when dealing with the
variety of character set mappings possible. LS1 and LS0 are the names
used in this chapter.
.P
Table 4-2 shows the equivalent 7-bit code extension for each 8-bit C1
code. The code extensions require one more byte than the C1 codes.
\fICHARACTER ENCODING\fR describes when to use C1 codes and when to use
7-bit code extensions. The \(lqcode extension announcers\(rq described
in \fICHARACTER ENCODING\fR are also listed in this chapter.
.
.SS "COMPATIBILITY LEVEL (DECSCL)"
