.SS "CHARACTER SET SELECTION (SCS)"
Character encoding in the VT220 was introduced in \fICHARACTER
ENCODING\fR. The control functions you need to select different graphic
character sets are described below. Differences with a VT100 terminal
are pointed out where they may affect software compatibility between the
VT220 and VT100-type terminals.
.P
The VT220's graphic character repertoire consists of the following
graphic sets (see \fICHARACTER ENCODING\fR for a description of these
character sets):
.P
.RS
.BPC "ASCII Graphics"
.BPC "DEC Supplemental Graphics"
.BPC "DEC Special Graphics"
.BPC "UK National"
.BPC "Down-line Loadable"
.RE
.P
Generally, you select character sets as follows.
.\" (Figure 4-1)
.P
First, using SCS sequences, you \(lqdesignate\(rq graphic sets as G0,
G1, G2, G3. This makes the graphics sets available \(lqon call\(rq for
your program. To actually map any of these sets into GL or GR you must
then \(lqinvoke\(rq any of G0 through G3 into GL or GR by using locking
shifts (LS0, LS1, LS2, LS1R, LS2R, LS3R) or single shifts (SS2, SS3).
.P
Character sets remain designated until the terminal receives another SCS
sequence. All locking shifts remain active until the terminal receives
another locking shift. Single shifts SS2 and SS3 remain active for only
the next single graphic character.
.P
You do not need to select character sets in this manner every time you
use the terminal because there is a default mapping. The default mapping
in VT200 mode is: ASCII Graphics in GL and DEC Supplemental Graphics in
GR (DEC Multinational). The default graphic character set mapping is
reset whenever you power up the terminal. Your application can also
select the default mapping by means of the soft terminal reset sequence
(DECSTR).
.P
All control functions for designating and invoking graphic character
sets are described below.
.
