.SS "SELECT C1 CONTROLS"
You can use \fISelect C1 Controls\fR (code extension announcers) in your
.\" the original text says "codes extension announcers"
program to control the representation of C1 control codes returned to
the application by the terminal (see \fICHARACTER ENCODING\fR for
information on working with 7- and 8-bit environments). The terminal
always accepts 7-bit or 8-bit forms for C1 controls in either of the
.\" original text: "[...] 7 or 8 bit forms [...]"
VT200 modes.
.P
Digital recommends that you use DECSCL sequences instead of Select C1
Controls because DECSCL performs a soft reset putting the terminal in a
\(lqknown\(rq state in addition to setting the terminal mode and C1
control state.
.P
.B NOTE:
.B These sequences are supported only in VT200 mode.
.
.SSS "Select 7-Bit C1 Control Transmission (S7C1T)"
.P
\&
.RS
.EX
.TS
l2 l1 l1
cB cB cB.
1/11	2/0	4/7
ESC	sp	F
.TE
.EE
.RE
.P
Causes all C1 codes returned to the application to be converted to their
equivalent 7-bit code extensions.
.P
.B NOTE:
.B The S7C1T sequence is ignored when the terminal is in VT100 or VT52
.B mode.
.
.SSS "Select 8-Bit C1 Control Transmission (S8C1T)"
.P
\&
.RS
.EX
.TS
l2 l1 l1
cB cB cB.
1/11	2/0	4/6
ESC	sp	G
.TE
.EE
.RE
.P
Causes the terminal to return C1 codes to the application without
converting them to their equivalent 7-bit code extensions.
.
.SS "TERMINAL MODES"
A mode is a state of the terminal that affects the way the terminal
operates. Modes that are selectable, and their set/reset control
sequences, are listed in Table 4-6 and described in this section. Each
mode has an identifying name (mnemonic), and each can be set or reset
individually or in strings using Set Mode (SM) or Reset Mode (RM)
control sequences. Certain features, called User Preference features,
can be \(lqlocked\(rq by the operator using Set-Up; thus preventing the
host from changing the feature.
.P
DIGITAL private control sequences (permitted within the extensions of
ANSI standards) are identified by DEC in the control sequence mnemonic,
which includes a question mark character (?) after the Control Sequence
Introducer.
.P
These modes are selectable by the terminal operator using Set-Up
screens.
.
