.SSS "Keyboard Action Mode (KAM)"
Keyboard Action mode lets your program lock and unlock the keyboard.
When the keyboard is locked, no codes can be transmitted from the
keyboard to the program. To alert the operator, whenever the keyboard is
locked, the \fBWAIT\fR indicator lights and the keyclick feature is
disabled. You select keyboard action mode by using the following
sequences.
.P
.B NOTE:
.B This is a User Preference feature and can be locked using Set-up.
.P
.TS
lB lB s s s lBX
l cT1 cT2 cT2 cT lTX
l cT  cT  cT  cT lTX
l cTB cTB cTB cT lTX
l cT  cT  cT  cT lTX
l cT  cT  cT  cT lTX
l cTB cTB cTB cT lTX.
Mode	Sequence	Action

Set	9/11	3/2	6/8		T{
Locks the keyboard for the next and subsequent keystrokes.
T}
	CSI	2	h		\^

Reset	9/11	3/2	6/12		T{
Unlocks the keyboard, unless it is locked by DC3.
T}
	CSI	2	l		\^
.TE
.
.SSS "Insert/Replacement Mode (IRM)"
The terminal displays received characters at the cursor position.
Insert/Replacement mode determines how the terminal adds characters to
the screen. Insert mode displays the character and moves previously
displayed characters to the right. Replace mode adds characters by
replacing the character at the cursor position. You select
Insert/Replacement Mode by using the following sequences.
.P
.TS
lB lB s s s lBX
l cT1 cT2 cT2 cT lTX
l cT  cT  cT  cT lTX
l cTB cTB cTB cT lTX
l cT  cT  cT  cT lTX
l cT  cT  cT  cT lTX
l cTB cTB cTB cT lTX.
Mode	Sequence	Action

Set	9/11	3/4	6/8		T{
Selects insert mode. New display characters move old display characters
to the right. Characters moved past the right margin are lost.
T}
	CSI	4	h		\^

Reset	9/11	3/4	6/12		T{
Selects replace mode. New display characters replace old display
characters at the cursor position. The old character is erased.
T}
	CSI	4	l		\^
.TE
.
.SSS "Send-Receive Mode (SRM)"
Send-Receive Mode turns local echo on or off. When send-receive mode is
reset (local echo on), every character transmitted from the keyboard
automatically appears on the screen. Therefore, the host does not have
to transmit (echo) the character back to the terminal display. When
send-receive mode is set (local echo off), the terminal transmits
characters only to the application. The host must echo the characters
back to the terminal display.
.P
.TS
lB lB s s s s lBX
l cT1 cT2 cT2 cT2 cT lTX
l cT  cT  cT  cT  cT lTX
l cTB cTB cTB cTB cT lTX
l cT  cT  cT  cT  cT lTX
l cT  cT  cT  cT  cT lTX
l cTB cTB cTB cTB cT lTX.
Mode	Sequence	Action

Set	9/11	3/1	3/2	6/8		T{
Turns off (disables) local echo. When the terminal transmits characters
to the host, the host must echo characters back to the terminal display.
T}
	CSI	1	2	h		\^

Reset	9/11	3/1	3/2	6/12		T{
Turns on (enables) local echo. When the terminal transmits characters,
the characters are automatically sent to the terminal display.
T}
	CSI	1	2	l		\^
.TE
.
.SSS "Line Feed/New Line Mode (LNM)"
Line feed/new line mode selects the control character(s) transmitted to
the application by the \fBRETURN\fR and \fBENTER\fR keys. \fBENTER\fR
transmits the same code as \fBRETURN\fR only when the auxiliary keypad
is in Keypad numeric mode (DECKPNM).
.P
Line feed/new line also selects the action taken by the terminal when
receiving line feed (LF), form feed (FF), or vertical tab (VT) codes.
These three codes are always processed identically.
.P
You set and reset linefeed/new line mode by using the following
sequences.
.P
.B NOTE:
.B For compatibility with DIGITAL software, this mode should always be
.B reset.
.P
.TS
lB lB s s s s lBX
l cT1 cT2 cT2 cT2 cT lTX
l cT  cT  cT  cT  cT lTX
l cTB cTB cTB cTB cT lTX
l cT  cT  cT  cT  cT lTX
l cT  cT  cT  cT  cT lTX
l cTB cTB cTB cTB cT lTX.
Mode	Sequence	Action

Set	9/11	3/2	3/0	6/8		T{
Causes a received LF, FF, or VT code to move the cursor to the first
column of the next line. \fBRETURN\fR transmits both a CR and a LF code.
T}
	CSI	2	0	h		\^

Reset	9/11	3/2	3/0	6/12		T{
Causes a received LF, FF, or VT code to move the cursor to the next line
in the current column. \fBRETURN\fR transmits a CR code only.
T}
	CSI	2	0	l		\^
.TE
.
.SSS "Text Cursor Enable Mode (DECTCEM)"
Text Cursor Enable Mode determines if the text cursor is visible.
.P
You set and reset this mode using the following sequences.
.P
.TS
lB lB s s s s s lBX
l cT1 cT1 cT2 cT2 cT2 cT lTX
l cT  cT  cT  cT  cT  cT lTX
l cTB cTB cTB cTB cTB cT lTX
l cT  cT  cT  cT  cT  cT lTX
l cT  cT  cT  cT  cT  cT lTX
l cTB cTB cTB cTB cTB cT lTX.
Mode	Sequence	Action

Set	9/11	3/15	3/2	3/5	6/8		T{
Causes the cursor to be visible.
T}
	CSI	?	2	5	h		\^

Reset	9/11	3/15	3/2	3/5	6/12		T{
Causes the cursor to not be visible.
T}
	CSI	?	2	5	l		\^
.TE
.
.SSS "Cursor Key Mode (DECCKM)"
The characters generated by the cursor keys depend on the state of
cursor key mode.
.P
You set and reset this mode using the following sequences.
.P
.TS
lB lB s s s s lBX
l cT1 cT1 cT2 cT2 cT lTX
l cT  cT  cT  cT  cT lTX
l cTB cTB cTB cTB cT lTX
l cT  cT  cT  cT  cT lTX
l cT  cT  cT  cT  cT lTX
l cTB cTB cTB cTB cT lTX.
Mode	Sequence	Action

Set	9/11	3/15	3/1	6/8		T{
Causes the cursor keys to generate \(lqapplication\(rq control
functions.
T}
	CSI	?	1	h		\^

Reset	9/11	3/15	3/1	6/12		T{
Causes the cursor keys to generate ANSI cursor control sequences.
T}
	CSI	?	1	l		\^
.TE
.
.SSS "ANSI/VT52 Mode (DECANM)"
In ANSI mode, reset selects VT52 compatibility mode. In VT52 mode, the
terminal responds to private DIGITAL sequences like a VT52. The reset
state of this mode sets the terminal to the VT52 mode. There is no Set
state for this mode.
.P
.\" NOTE: the original manual has a broken table here. This table format
.\" is taken from the VT240 Reference Manual instead.
.TS
lB lB s s s s lBX
l cT1 cT2 cT2 cT2 cT lTX
l cT  cT  cT  cT  cT lTX
l cTB cTB cTB cTB cT lTX.
Mode	Sequence	Action

Reset	9/11	3/15	3/2	6/12		T{
Sets the terminal to VT52 mode.
T}
	CSI	?	2	l		\^
.TE
.
.SSS "Column Mode (DECCOLM)"
Column mode selects the number of columns per line (80 or 132) on the
display. The screen can display 24 lines of text with either selection.
You select the number of columns per line by using the following
sequences.
.P
.B NOTE:
.B When the terminal receives the sequence, the screen is erased and the
.B cursor moves to the home position. This also sets the scrolling
.B region for full screen (24 lines).
.P
.TS
lB lB s s s s lBX
l cT1 cT1 cT2 cT2 cT lTX
l cT  cT  cT  cT  cT lTX
l cTB cTB cTB cTB cT lTX
l cT  cT  cT  cT  cT lTX
l cT  cT  cT  cT  cT lTX
l cTB cTB cTB cTB cT lTX.
Mode	Sequence	Action

Set	9/11	3/15	3/3	6/8		T{
Selects 132 columns per line.
T}
	CSI	?	3	h		\^

Reset	9/11	3/15	3/3	6/12		T{
Selects 80 columns per line.
T}
	CSI	?	3	l		\^
.TE
.
.SSS "Scrolling Mode (DECSCLM)"
Scrolling is the upward or downward movement of existing lines on the
screen. There are two methods of scrolling, jump scroll and smooth
scroll (6 lines per second). You select the scroll mode (smooth or jump)
using the following sequences.
.P
.B NOTE:
.B This is a User Preference feature and can be locked using Set-up.
.P
.TS
lB lB s s s s lBX
l cT1 cT1 cT2 cT2 cT lTX
l cT  cT  cT  cT  cT lTX
l cTB cTB cTB cTB cT lTX
l cT  cT  cT  cT  cT lTX
l cT  cT  cT  cT  cT lTX
l cTB cTB cTB cTB cT lTX.
Mode	Sequence	Action

Set	9/11	3/15	3/4	6/8		T{
Selects smooth scroll. Smooth scroll lets the terminal add no more than
6 lines per second to the screen.
T}
	CSI	?	4	h		\^

Reset	9/11	3/15	3/4	6/12		T{
Selects jump scroll. Jump scroll lets the terminal add lines to the
screen as fast as possible.
T}
	CSI	?	4	l		\^
.TE
.
.SSS "Screen Mode (DECSCNM)"
Screen mode selects either a dark or light (reverse) display background
on the screen.
.P
.B NOTE:
.B This is a User Preference feature and can be \(lqlocked\(rq by the
.B terminal operator using Set-Up.
.P
.TS
lB lB s s s s lBX
l cT1 cT1 cT2 cT2 cT lTX
l cT  cT  cT  cT  cT lTX
l cTB cTB cTB cTB cT lTX
l cT  cT  cT  cT  cT lTX
l cT  cT  cT  cT  cT lTX
l cTB cTB cTB cTB cT lTX.
Mode	Sequence	Action

Set	9/11	3/15	3/5	6/8		T{
Selects reverse video (dark characters on a light background).
T}
	CSI	?	5	h		\^

Reset	9/11	3/15	3/5	6/12		T{
Selects normal screen (light characters on a dark background).
T}
	CSI	?	5	l		\^
.TE
.
.SSS "Origin Mode (DECOM)"
Origin mode allows cursor addressing relative to a user-defined origin.
This mode resets when the terminal is powered up or reset. It does not
affect the erase in display (ED) function.
.P
.TS
lB lB s s s s lBX
l cT1 cT1 cT2 cT2 cT lTX
l cT  cT  cT  cT  cT lTX
l cTB cTB cTB cTB cT lTX
l cT  cT  cT  cT  cT lTX
l cT  cT  cT  cT  cT lTX
l cTB cTB cTB cTB cT lTX.
Mode	Sequence	Action

Set	9/11	3/15	3/6	6/8		T{
Select home position with line numbers starting at top margin of the
user-defined scrolling region. The cursor cannot move out of the
scrolling region.
T}
	CSI	?	6	h		\^

Reset	9/11	3/15	3/6	6/12		T{
Select home position in upper-left corner of the screen. Line numbers
are independent of the scrolling region. Use the CUP sequence to move
the cursor out of the scrolling region.
T}
	CSI	?	6	l		\^
.TE
.
.SSS "Auto Wrap Mode (DECAWM)"
This mode selects where received graphic characters appear when the
cursor is at the right margin.
.P
.B NOTE:
.B Regardless of this selection, the tab character never moves the
.B cursor to the next line.
.P
.TS
lB lB s s s s lBX
l cT1 cT1 cT2 cT2 cT lTX
l cT  cT  cT  cT  cT lTX
l cTB cTB cTB cTB cT lTX
l cT  cT  cT  cT  cT lTX
l cT  cT  cT  cT  cT lTX
l cTB cTB cTB cTB cT lTX.
Mode	Sequence	Action

Set	9/11	3/15	3/7	6/8		T{
Selects auto wrap. Graphic display characters received when the cursor
is at the right margin appear on the next line. The display scrolls up
if the cursor is at the end of the scrolling region.
T}
	CSI	?	7	h		\^

Reset	9/11	3/15	3/7	6/12		T{
Turns off auto wrap. Graphic display characters received when the cursor
is at the right margin replace previously displayed characters.
T}
	CSI	?	7	l		\^
.TE
.
.SSS "Auto Repeat (DECARM)"
Auto repeat mode selects automatic key repeating. When auto repeat mode
is set, a key pressed for more than 0.5 seconds automatically repeats
the transmission of the character. The following keys never autorepeat:
.BR "HOLD SCREEN" ,
.BR "PRINT SCREEN" ,
.BR "SET-UP" ,
.BR "DATA/TALK" ,
.BR "BREAK" ,
.BR "RETURN" ,
.BR "COMPOSE CHARACTER" ,
.BR "LOCK" ,
.BR "SHIFT" ,
.BR "CTRL" .
You select auto repeat mode using the following sequences.
.P
.B NOTE:
.B This is a User Preference feature and can be \(lqlocked\(rq by the
.B operator using Set-up.
.P
.TS
lB lB s s s s lBX
l cT1 cT1 cT2 cT2 cT lTX
l cT  cT  cT  cT  cT lTX
l cTB cTB cTB cTB cT lTX
l cT  cT  cT  cT  cT lTX
l cT  cT  cT  cT  cT lTX
l cTB cTB cTB cTB cT lTX.
Mode	Sequence	Action

Set	9/11	3/15	3/8	6/8		T{
Selects auto repeat mode. A key pressed for more than 0.5 seconds
automatically repeats.
T}
	CSI	?	8	h		\^

Reset	9/11	3/15	3/8	6/12		T{
Turns off auto repeat. Keys pressed do not automatically repeat.
T}
	CSI	?	8	l		\^
.TE
.
.SSS "Print Form Feed Mode (DECPFF)"
This mode determines if the terminal transmits a print termination
character after a screen print. The form feed control character (FF)
serves as the print termination character. You select print form feed
mode using the following sequences.
.P
.TS
lB lB s s s s s lBX
l cT1 cT1 cT2 cT2 cT2 cT lTX
l cT  cT  cT  cT  cT  cT lTX
l cTB cTB cTB cTB cTB cT lTX
l cT  cT  cT  cT  cT  cT lTX
l cT  cT  cT  cT  cT  cT lTX
l cTB cTB cTB cTB cTB cT lTX.
Mode	Sequence	Action

Set	9/11	3/15	3/1	3/8	6/8		T{
Selects form feed (FF) as print termination character. The terminal
transmits this character to the printer after each print screen
operation.
T}
	CSI	?	1	8	h		\^

Reset	9/11	3/15	3/1	3/8	6/12		T{
Select no termination character. The terminal does not transmit a form
feed (FF) to the printer after each print screen operation.
T}
	CSI	?	1	8	l		\^
.TE
.
.SSS "Print Extent Mode (DECPEX)"
Print extent mode selects the full screen or the scrolling region to
print during a print screen operation. You select this mode using the
following sequences.
.P
.TS
lB lB s s s s s lBX
l cT1 cT1 cT2 cT2 cT2 cT lTX
l cT  cT  cT  cT  cT  cT lTX
l cTB cTB cTB cTB cTB cT lTX
l cT  cT  cT  cT  cT  cT lTX
l cT  cT  cT  cT  cT  cT lTX
l cTB cTB cTB cTB cTB cT lTX.
Mode	Sequence	Action

Set	9/11	3/15	3/1	3/9	6/8		T{
Selects full screen to print during a print screen operation.
T}
	CSI	?	1	9	h		\^

Reset	9/11	3/15	3/1	3/9	6/12		T{
Selects scrolling region to print during a print screen operation.
T}
	CSI	?	1	9	l		\^
.TE
.
.SSS "Keypad Mode (DECKPAM/DECKPNM)"
.\" the original manual incorrectly calls this "DECPNM" here
The auxiliary keypad generates either numeric characters or control
functions. Selecting application or numeric keypad mode determines the
type of characters.
.P
.B NOTE:
.B When the terminal is powered up or reset, the terminal selects
.B numeric keypad mode.
.P
.TS
lB lB s s lBTX
l cT1 cT1 cT lTX
l cT  cT  cT lTX
l cBT cBT cT lTX
l cT  cT  cT lTX
l cT  cT  cT lTX
l cBT cBT cT lTX.
Mode	Sequence	Action

Application	1/11	3/13		T{
Selects application keypad mode. Keypad generates \(lqapplication\(rq
control functions.
T}
(DECKPAM)	ESC	\&=		\^

Numeric	1/11	3/14		T{
Selects numeric keypad mode. Keypad generates characters that match the
numeric, comma, period, and minus sign keys on main keypad. \fBPF1\fR
through \fBPF4\fR generate control functions.
T}
(DECKPNM)	ESC	>		\^
.TE
.
.SS "CURSOR POSITIONING"
The cursor indicates the active screen position where the next character
will appear in the absence of auto wrap. A number of operations
implicitly affect cursor positioning. In addition, you can control
cursor movement using the following sequences.
.P
.B NOTE:
.B Pn is a variable, ASCII encoded, numeric parameter. If you select no
.B parameter or a parameter value of 0, the terminal assumes the
.B parameter equals 1.
.P
.TS
lB lB s s s s lBX
lT cT1 cT1 cT1 cT1 cT  lTX
lT cT  cT  cT  cT  cT  lTX
lT cTB cTB cTB cTB cTB lTX
lT cT  cT  cT  cT  cT  lTX
lT cT  cT  cT  cT  cT  lTX
lT cTB cTB cTB cTB cTB lTX
lT cT  cT  cT  cT  cT  lTX
lT cT  cT  cT  cT  cT  lTX
lT cTB cTB cTB cTB cTB lTX
lT cT  cT  cT  cT  cT  lTX
lT cT  cT  cT  cT  cT  lTX
lT cTB cTB cTB cTB cTB lTX
lT cT  cT  cT  cT  cT  lTX
lT cT  cT  cT  cT  cT  lTX
lT cTB cTB cTB cTB cTB lTX
lT cT  cT  cT  cT  cT  lTX
lT cT  cT  cT  cT  cT  lTX
lT cTB cTB cTB cTB cTB lTX
lT cT  cT  cT  cT  cT  lTX
lT cT  cT  cT  cT  cT  lTX
lT cTB cTB cTB cTB cTB lTX
lT cT  cT  cT  cT  cT  lTX
lT cT  cT  cT  cT  cT  lTX
lT cTB cTB cTB cTB cTB lTX
lT cT  cT  cT  cT  cT  lTX
lT cT  cT  cT  cT  cT  lTX
lT cTB cTB cTB cTB cTB lTX
lT cT  cT  cT  cT  cT  lTX
lT cT  cT  cT  cT  cT  lTX
lT cTB cTB cTB cTB cTB lTX
lT cT  cT  cT  cT  cT  lTX
lT cT  cT  cT  cT  cT  lTX
lT cTB cTB cTB cTB cTB lTX.
Name	Sequence	Action

Cursor Up	9/11		4/1			T{
Moves the cursor up Pn lines in the same column. The cursor stops at the
top margin.
T}
(CUU)	CSI	Pn	A			\^

Cursor Down	9/11		4/2			T{
Moves the cursor down Pn lines in the same column. The cursor stops at
the bottom margin.
T}
(CUD)	CSI	Pn	B			\^

Cursor Forward	9/11		4/3			T{
Moves the cursor right Pn columns. The cursor stops at the right margin.
T}
(CUF)	CSI	Pn	C			\^

Cursor Backward	9/11		4/4			T{
Moves the cursor left Pn columns. The cursor stops at the left margin.
T}
(CUB)	CSI	Pn	D			\^

Cursor Position	9/11		3/11		4/8	T{
Moves the cursor to line Pl, column Pc. The numbering of the lines and
columns depends on the state (set/reset) of origin mode (DECOM).
T}
(CUP)	CSI	Pl	;	Pc	H	\^

T{
Horizontal And Vertical Position (HVP)
T}	9/11		3/11		6/6	T{
Moves the cursor to line Pl, column Pc. The numbering of the lines and
columns depends on the state (set/reset) of origin mode (DECOM). DIGITAL
recommends using CUP instead of HVP.
T}
\^	CSI	Pl	;	Pc	f	\^

Index (IND)	1/11	4/4				T{
IND is an 8-bit control character (8/4). It can be expressed as an
escape sequence for a 7-bit environment. IND moves the cursor down one
line in the same column. If the cursor is at the bottom margin the
screen performs a scroll-up.
T}
	ESC	D				\^

T{
Reverse Index (RI)
T}	1/11	4/13				T{
RI is an 8-bit control character (8/13). It can be expressed as an
escape sequence for a 7-bit environment. RI moves the cursor up one line
in the same column. If the cursor is at the top margin the screen
performs a scroll-down.
T}
\^	ESC	M				\^

T{
Next Line (NEL)
T}	1/11	4/5				T{
NEL is an 8-bit control character (8/5). It can be expressed as an
escape sequence for a 7-bit environment. NEL moves the cursor to the
first position on the next line. If the cursor is at the bottom margin
the screen performs a scroll-up.
T}
\^	ESC	E				\^

Save Cursor	1/11	3/7				T{
Saves in terminal memory the:
.BPC "cursor position"
.BPC "graphic rendition"
.BPC "character set shift state"
.BPC "state of wrap flag"
.BPC "state of origin mode"
.BPC "state of selective erase"
T}
(DECSC)	ESC	7				\^

Restore Cursor	1/11	3/8				T{
Restores the states described for (DECSC) above. If none of the
characteristics were saved: the cursor moves to home position, origin
mode is reset, no character attributes are assigned, and the default
character set mapping is established.
T}
(DECRC)	ESC	8				\^
.TE
.
.SS "TAB STOPS"
You select tab stop positions on the horizontal lines of the screen. The
cursor advances (tabs) to the next tab stop when the terminal receives a
horizontal tab code (HT, 0/9). If there is no next tab, HT moves the
cursor to the right margin. You set and clear the tab stops using the
following sequences.
.P
.B NOTE:
.B These sequences are affected by the User Preference Lock in Set-up.
.P
.TS
lB lB s s lBX
l c1 c1 c  lX
l c  c  c  lX
l cB cB cB lX
l c  c  c  lX
l c  c  c  lX
l cB cB cB lX
l c  c  c  lX
l c  c  c  lX
l cB cB cB lX
l c  c  c  lX
l c  c  c  lX
l cB cB cB lX.
Name	Sequence	Action

Horizontal	1/11	4/8		T{
HTS sets a tab stop at the current column. HTS is an 8-bit control
character (8/8) that you can also express as an escape sequence when
coding for a 7-bit environment.
T}
Tab Set (HTS)	ESC	H		\^

Tabulation	9/11	6/7		T{
Clears a horizontal tab stop at cursor position.
T}
Clear (TBC)	CSI	g		\^

	9/11	3/0	6/7	T{
Clears a horizontal tab stop at cursor position.
T}
	CSI	0	g	\^

	9/11	3/3	6/7	T{
Clears all horizontal tab stops.
T}
	CSI	3	g	\^
.TE
.
.SS "CHARACTER RENDITION AND ATTRIBUTES"
Character rendition and attributes are display features that affect the
way a character is displayed without changing the character. You change
character rendition using Select Graphics Rendition (SGR) sequences. You
can also elect to designate characters to be selective erasable or not
selective erasable when using erase sequences using the DECSCA sequence.
.
.SSS "Select Graphic Rendition (SGR)"
You can select one or more character renditions at a time using the
following format:
.P
.RS
.EX
.TS
c1 c1 c1 c1 c1 c1
cB cB cB cB cB cB.
9/11		3/11			6/13
CSI	Ps	;	Ps	...	m
.TE
.EE
.RE
.P
When you use multiple parameters, they are executed in sequence. The
