.SS "SCROLLING MARGINS (TOP AND BOTTOM)"
The scrolling region is the area of the screen that can receive new
characters by scrolling old characters off the screen. The area is
defined by the top and bottom screen margins. The minimum size of the
scrolling region allowed is two lines; therefore the number of the top
margin must be at least one less than the number of the bottom margin.
You select the top and bottom margins of the scrolling region by using
the following sequence.
.
.SSS "Set Top and Bottom Margins (DECSTBM)"
.P
\&
.RS
.EX
.TS
c1 c1 c1 c1 c1
cB cB cB cB cB.
9/11		3/11		7/2
CSI	Pt	;	Pb	r
.TE
.EE
.RE
.P
This sequence selects top and bottom margins defining the scrolling
region. Pt is the line number of the first line in the scrolling region.
Pb is the line number of the bottom line. If either Pt or Pb is not
selected, they default to top and bottom respectively. Lines are counted
from \(lq1\(rq.
.P
.B NOTE:
.B Power up or reset causes the scrolling region to be the complete
.B screen.
.
.SS "PRINTING"
All print operations are selectable via control sequences. When
characters are printed on the screen, terminal and printer tab stops are
ignored. Print characters are spaced with the space (SP) character. The
terminal transmits a carriage return (CR) and line feed (LF), or
vertical tab (VT) or form feed (FF) after the last printable character
of a line (not a space character).
.P
.B NOTE:
.B Spaces with video attributes are \(lqprintable\(rq characters.
.P
Before you select a print operation, you should check printer status
using the print status report (DSR) (see Reports section). You select
print operations using the following sequences.
.P
.TS
