.P
For example, suppose you want to design character A. To do this, you
designate which pixels are to be on and which pixels are to be off. Your
design may look like what is shown in Figure 4-5.
.P
After you have established what your DRCS character A is going to look
like, you then divide the pixels of the DRCS character cell into columns
of six bits each using the format shown in Figure 4-6. The column
numbers here designate the order in which the columns will be sent to
the terminal. Each column is now represented as a vertical 1 \*[times] 6
pixel matrix called a \(lqsixel\(rq with the least significant bit at
the top and the most significant bit at the bottom. Because the
character height (10 pixels) is not a multiple of six, the columns on
the bottom of the character cell consist of only four bits each (the two
highest order bits, 5 and 6, are ignored).
.
