.P
After you have divided your DRCS character into six-pixel columns
(\(lqsixels\(rq), you then convert the binary values of each column to
its equivalent character. Because column codes are restricted to
characters with the range of ? (octal 077) to \(ti (octal 176), you must
add an offset of octal 077 to each column octal value. Thus, binary
value 000000 is converted to octal 077 (octal 0 + octal 77); binary
110101 is converted to octal 164 (octal 65 + octal 077); and binary
111111 is converted to octal 176 (octal 077 + 077).
.P
After you have converted the binary column codes to octal values (using
the offset), you then convert the resultant octal value for each column
to its equivalent character using the ASCII table in \fICHARACTER
ENCODING\fR. Figure 4-7 provides this conversion procedure for our
example DRCS character A.
.
