.IP * 2
Applies only to subsequent restore cursor commands (DECRC)
.
.SSS "Hard Terminal Reset (RIS)"
RIS (hard terminal reset or reset-to-initial state) can be invoked
anytime by the terminal operator using RECALL in Set-Up. It can also be
invoked from the host computer anytime via an escape sequence. RIS
causes an NVR recall. All Set-Up parameters are replaced by their NVR
values, or power-up default values if NVR values do not exist.
.P
In addition, RIS:
.bP
performs a communications line disconnect
.bP
clears UDKs
.bP
clears a down-line loaded character set
.bP
clears the screen
.bP
returns the cursor to the upper-left corner on the screen
.bP
sets the SGR state to normal
.bP
sets the selective erase attribute write state to \(lqnon-selective
erasable\(rq
.P
The RIS escape sequence is:
.P
.RS
.EX
.TS
c1 c1
cB cB.
1/11	6/3
ESC	c
.TE
.EE
.RE
.P
.B NOTE:
.B This sequence should be used with caution. Parity and baud rate are
.B restored from NVR.
.
.SS "TESTS AND ADJUSTMENTS (DECTST AND DECALN)"
The terminal has tests and alignment patterns that can be invoked from
the keyboard or the host computer via control and escape sequences. Test
and alignment procedures are usually performed only by DIGITAL
manufacturing and Field Service personnel.
.P
This section provides the sequences used to invoke the tests and the
alignment patterns. For detailed information, however, refer to the
VT220 Pocket Service Guide.
.
.SSS "Tests (DECTST)"
The sequence format for invoking terminal tests is:
.P
.RS
.EX
.TS
c2 c3 c1 c2 c0 c0 c2 c2 c
cB cB cB cB cB cB cB cB cB.
9/11	3/4	3/11		3/11		3/11		7/9
CSI	4	;	Ps	;	.....	;	Ps	y
.TE
.EE
.RE
.P
Each Ps is a parameter indicating a test to be performed. After the
.\" original manual: [...] is the parameter [...]"
first parameter (4), the parameters each select one test from the
following list. Several tests can be invoked at one time by chaining the
parameters together, separated by semicolons. The tests are not
necessarily executed in the order in which they are entered.
.P
.B NOTE:
.B DECTST causes a communications line disconnect.
.P
.TS
lB lBX
l lX.
Parameter	Test

0	Test 1, 2, 3, and 6
1	Power-Up Self Test
2	EIA Port Data Loopback Test
3	Printer Port Loopback Test
4	(not used)
5	(not used)
6	EIA Port Modem Control Line Loopback Test
7	20 mA Port Loopback Test
8	(not used)
9	T{
Repeat any selected test continuously until power off or failure
10 and up	(not used)
T}
.TE
.
.SSS "Adjustments (DECALN)"
The terminal has a screen alignment pattern that service personnel use
to adjust the screen. You can display the screen alignment pattern using
the DECALN sequence.
.P
.RS
.EX
.TS
c1 c1 c1
cB cB cB.
1/11	2/3	3/8
ESC	#	8
.TE
.EE
.RE
.P
This sequence fills the screen with upper case E's.
.
.SS "VT52 MODE ESCAPE SEQUENCES"
The VT52 mode allows the VT220 to operate with DIGITAL software written
for the VT52 terminal. In VT52 mode, all C0 control characters are
allowed, although some are ignored. No C1 control characters or ANSI
mode control functions are allowed. The user-defined keys are disabled.
Table 4-11 defines the VT52 mode escape sequences. Table 3-3 in
\fITRANSMITTED CODES\fR defines the VT52 auxiliary keypad codes. The
set-up VT100 (ASCII/UK) default character set applies to VT52 mode as
well.
.
.TBL "4-11" "VT52 Escape Sequences"
.TS
lB lB
l l.
_
Escape Sequence	Function
ESC A	Cursor Up
ESC B	Cursor Down
ESC C	Cursor Right
ESC D	Cursor Left
ESC F	Enter \(lqGraphics\(rq Mode
ESC G	Exit \(qlGraphics\(rq Mode
ESC H	Cursor To Home
ESC I	Reverse Line Feed
ESC J	Erase To End Of Screen
ESC K	Erase To End Of Line
ESC Y Line Column	Direct Cursor Address
ESC Z	Identify
ESC \&=	Enter Alternate Keypad Mode
ESC >	Exit Alternate Keypad Mode
ESC <	Enter ANSI Mode
ESC \(ha	Enter Auto Print Mode
ESC _	Exit Auto Print Mode
ESC W	Enter Printer Controller Mode
ESC X	Exit Printer Controller Mode
ESC ]	Print Screen
ESC V	Print Cursor Line
_
.TE
.
.
